<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Ball</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1338–1381</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Ball was an English priest whose preaching on the equality of all people made him one of the leaders of the Peasants' Revolt of 1381. Excommunicated and imprisoned several times for sermons attacking the wealth of the Church and the lords, he was freed from Maidstone prison by the Kentish rebels and preached to them at Blackheath on the text 'When Adam delved and Eve span, who was then the gentleman?'. After the revolt collapsed he was captured at Coventry and executed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-albans">St Albans</a> on 15 July 1381.</p></div>
